  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $14$ and $8$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $14,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{14}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{8}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{14}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{14}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{14+8}={x}^{22}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $22.$",
